plan Runtime
title   Applications, readiness, actors and context
status  proposed
goal
    Flow has one Runtime, and every host runs on it: a browser's React tree, a server request, a
    Story and a CLI command.

    It gives each of them the same guarantees: a closed set of machines, one store, one ordered
    decision path per actor, causally complete context and truthful cleanup.

    **Why now:** every host runs the same machines, so ordering, storage, context and cleanup must behave the same in
    each. This Plan fixes the one Runtime they share; other Plans own the parts it runs: turns (@plan.Machines.O3),
    operations (@plan.Operations.O1), storage (@plan.Persistence.O1), React (@plan.React.O1), hydration
    (@plan.ServerRendering.O1) and lazy entries (@plan.LazyFeatures.O1).

    **Read first:** @spec.Actors and @spec.Compile, the Specs built from this Plan; the reference
    app code:workflow/plans/flow/references/dispatch-desk/app/app.ts.
outcomes
    O1  Apps
        app({ id, machines }) is inert and lists every machine the Runtime may run. An entry is a
        Machine, { machine, context? }, or a lazy { id, load, context? } (@plan.LazyFeatures.O1).
        The list is closed for the Runtime's lifetime: no runtime registration or child actors.

        Construction throws FlowError on unknown fields, duplicate machine ids, one machine under
        two keys, and colliding operation, domain or entity identities; lazy entries are checked the
        same way when adopted, before any work. Constructing an app, setup or ref runs no callback,
        loader, service, storage access, actor, timer or evidence.

        machine() is the compile: its output, Compiled, is plain data plus Slots, each closure named
        by its place in the definition (operations.pay.failure.CardDeclined), and the Runtime boots
        it in the same process; there is no runtime codec for Compiled. Tools read its data view,
        whose closures are names.

        Compiled carries every event with its payload Schemas, the Flow.uncertain events, each
        memory field (its Schema, its one source, private), each operation's key fields, input
        fields and services, and each fragment's kind, events and ports, so flow check, flow graph,
        the Story stand-in host and the admission door read data, never bodies.

        An operation's key is made from its key fields alone; a key field outside the input, a
        missing events table, or a function in Compiled that is not a Slot is refused when the
        Runtime is created. The details are agent-judged internal representation.

        A machine entry's context is the default wiring for every actor of that machine; a shared
        ref may override declared slots. The resolved wiring must cover every declared slot with a
        compatible provider. Call sites (ensure, create, hooks) never supply wiring. Durable
        identity is app id, machine id and the actor's identity (@O3), never an app property key.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/app/app.ts.
    O2  Setup and readiness
        A Runtime is built without side effects and becomes ready through one shared bootstrap that
        reads storage before any app service starts.

        runtimeSetup({ app, services?, scope?, persistence?, repairSlots?, audit? }).construct() is
        synchronous and inert. scope is an optional nonempty string naming whose data the Runtime
        holds, such as a user, workspace or tenant id. It partitions saved data
        (@plan.Persistence.O1) and stamps and checks hydration payloads (@plan.ServerRendering.O6);
        machines, operations and conditions never read it. `repairSlots` (default 6) and audit
        (default false, for development and tests only) are @plan.EntityDomains.O5 and
        @plan.EntityDomains.O4. services is an Effect Layer typed by the machines' R: it must
        provide every service the app's contracts list in their services, lazy entries included; a
        missing service is a type error, and the boot refuses, by machine, operation and service, an
        operation whose service it lacks, so no write is marked unknown for a missing service.

        Each service is acquired once per Runtime.

        ready() is the one readiness Effect; FlowProvider calls it in browsers (@plan.React.O1).
        The first call starts the bootstrap; concurrent and later callers share its result,
        including a failure (no implicit retry). Interrupting one waiter interrupts only that wait.
        Bootstrap order:

        1. With persistence, claim scope ownership (@plan.Persistence.O7).
        2. Acquire storage, then read and validate persisted data before any app service starts
           (@plan.Persistence.O5), upgrading Flow's record formats and resetting records whose saved
           shape changed incompatibly.
        3. Merge restored data with any hydrated items (@plan.ServerRendering.O5).
        4. Acquire the remaining services, install hydrated actors and the restored identities owed
           an ending (a lane InFlight at capture, or a parked ending), restored live as work in
           flight (providers before consumers), keep every other restored record a record until an
           ensure, a subscription or a send reaches it (@plan.Persistence.O4), establish silent
           context baselines, then open the Runtime to callers. Transferred lazy entries load after
           ready resolves (@plan.LazyFeatures.O4).

        A service acquisition failure fails ready() with FlowError and its Cause, releases what was
        acquired in reverse order, and leaves no partial actor or evidence. Before ready succeeds,
        `ensureActor` and `createActor` reject with RuntimeNotReady; React's pre-attach handles
        (@plan.React.O2) are the only exception.

        A send to a persisted actor before boot queues until boot, within Persistence O5's bound;
        past the bound, or on a failed read, it reports StorageFailure, the Runtime runs without
        persistence and the queue is flushed; a late answer is ignored.

        Disposing during bootstrap cancels it and closes admission; a service acquired late is
        released and no handle is ever exposed. runtime.runPromise runs an Effect with the installed
        services; there is no `runPromiseExit`. Reference: references/dispatch-desk/app/setup.ts,
        app/host.ts, app/persistence.ts.
    O3  Actor identity and lifetime
        An actor is its machine plus its input. actorRef(machine, nameOrValue, { context? }) is
        inert and names a shared actor in one of two forms. From a value: the value carries the
        machine's Flow.input fields, which are its identity; Flow keeps only those fields, and
        ensuring it takes no input (ensureActor(ref)). A singleton: a literal string name (a runtime
        string is a type error).

        The form is fixed per machine in the types: a machine with input fields is named only by a
        value, a machine with no input only by a literal name, so a singleton takes no input. The
        identity encodes the input fields with their Schemas, in field-name order, as strings, so a
        reorder changes nothing. persist is the machine's, declared on its definition
        (@plan.Persistence.O2); refs carry no persist.

        Two refs of one identity with different context reject with ConflictingActorRef and change
        nothing. A local actor (runtime.createActor, useActor, Story.machine) is (machine, input by
        Equal.equals). Inspection, flow trace and Story records name an actor by machine and fields,
        Editor{workspaceId: "north", ticketId: "T-42"}, or a singleton's name.

        runtime.ensureActor(ref) returns the one shared actor for that identity, creating it from
        its input, or restoring it from the Runtime's copy of its record.

        A failed admission leaves nothing behind, so a later ensure may retry.
        runtime.createActor(machine, { input }) always creates a new local actor that cannot persist
        or be a send target and lives until disposed.

        Handles expose send, `getSnapshot` and snapshots; a handle follows the identity, not the
        incarnation. `getSnapshot` reads the live incarnation; with none, a persisted identity's
        record (lifecycle active; a record that does not decode reads quarantined with its issue);
        with neither, the pre-attach snapshot if the identity never had an incarnation in this
        Runtime, otherwise the last incarnation's terminal snapshot.

        A shared actor lives while it is used, and until its finite work in flight has returned. A
        use is an attached consumer (from its passive commit effect, never from render), a live
        snapshots subscription, a bound context consumer, or Flow's hold for dehydrate and Hydrate;
        uses count per identity. `ensureActor` alone, `getSnapshot`, lookups, views and sends are
        not uses.

        Work in flight is an InFlight write lane, a read started in a turn that handles the ending
        of work in flight (transitively), and an ending owed at ready; it keeps the actor until its
        ending's turn has run, so a chained step is committed by the incarnation that started it.

        Nothing else keeps an actor: not schedules, other reads, placements, stream sessions, Held
        lanes, parked endings or context; queued mail is processed before the end. While nothing
        uses it, its schedules wait (@plan.Machines.O5).

        Free of both for the grace (100 ms on the Runtime Clock, internal, the same for local
        actors), it ends: a persisted actor's record is encoded in that step (the end waits while it
        cannot be encoded), its lanes move to the Runtime unbound, its schedules are cancelled, its
        placements, sessions and unkept reads stop, and each read it stops parks failure {
        Interrupted } for the identity; when that stops live work, the development warning
        EndedWithWork names it once per machine and kind of work.

        An ensure while it is ending waits and binds the next incarnation; disposing and then
        ensuring the same ref creates a new incarnation. Host `createActor` actors live until
        disposed. retain does not exist: an actor that must outlive a screen is used from where it
        should live.

        A persisted identity returns, when an ensure, a subscription or a send reaches it, as a new
        incarnation with a newly minted id, restored from the Runtime's copy; it receives its
        starting turn, its lanes' deliveries, the Interrupted endings of reads its end stopped, one
        `onContext` wave if a provider's selection changed, then the event that restored it.

        An ending owned by the previous incarnation is delivered as adopted, current from the active
        leaf. A non-persisted identity starts fresh. An ensure over a record that no longer decodes
        starts fresh, reports RecordUndecodable (evidence, proposed name) and adopts the identity's
        lanes.

        A local actor remounted within its grace reuses that actor; a new incarnation of an equal
        local identity starting while an older one is kept only by work in flight disposes the older
        one and adopts its lanes.

        A write lane is (actor identity with its version, contract id, key), owned by the Runtime.

        A starting incarnation adopts only its own identity's unbound lanes, the oldest first for
        equal local identities, and receives, as follow-up turns before any external event, the
        parked endings in saved order, then each held head's unknown { Restored } once per
        incarnation, then an in-flight Attempt's ending when it lands.

        A lane records the creating incarnation's subject path, and its binding incarnation is the
        live incarnation with that path (the actor for a definition's operation); a member from
        another path is declined; with no binding incarnation in a live actor its ending is parked
        for the path. No schedule, issue or send from an old incarnation reaches a new one.

        Versions.

        For a persisted value-named machine, adding, removing or renaming an input field, or
        changing a field's Schema on its type side or encoded side, is a new version; a check
        compares by its representation { id, payload } and other annotations are ignored (a brand or
        a description is not a version; a custom filter with no representation, and a transformation
        with unchanged sides, are not seen: stated limits).

        - Flow's own version encodings carry a format tag;
        - Flow keeps each older format's hash function and reads an older-format version by
          recomputing the current Schema in that format;
        - format 0 (names only) compares names, a stated limit;
        - a newer format reads as an old version;
        - production tags versions from its first release (the runner's rulings).

        A saved record of another version is an old-version record, listed at ready
        (OldVersionActor, proposed name), never renamed or matched by shared fields, removed by
        forget; its non-Idle lanes stay unbound and listed (@plan.Persistence.O5). Reference:
        references/dispatch-desk/features/Tickets/editor/editor.ref.ts, app/host.ts.
    O4  Mailboxes and send
        Each actor processes events one at a time; its mailbox is FIFO in admission order, and user
        sends, call endings, stream deliveries, timer firings and `onContext` events queue together.
        A call's ending is queued at settlement and mapped and routed when its turn is admitted.
        Equal deadlines are separate turns.

        Across actors, follow-ups run first, up to 64 for each admitted event, past which the turn
        that would add one is refused InvalidTurn naming the chain; otherwise one event from the
        next actor in rotation, oldest first within that actor. Nothing orders two actors' turns
        unless a turn links them, so bulk work on one actor never starves another.

        A send to an idle Runtime runs its turn before send returns; during a turn, or a yield
        between slices, it queues. Work runs in slices: the driver serves mail for a slice of time,
        then yields a real task to the page; the slice decides when, never what.

        A spin, a handler that asks again on its own ending, is never stopped, slowed or cut;
        TurnStorm (evidence) names one operation failing with the same input many times in a row,
        and flow check's AsksForever a handler whose every path asks again; neither decides
        anything.

        Each actor queues at most 1,024 pending events: an external send beyond that throws
        AdmissionOverloaded and changes nothing. send validates the event's tag and payload; an
        internal, unknown or invalid event throws InvalidEvent and starts no turn.

        Endings, schedule firings and context updates are never dropped: they are bounded by work
        already admitted, not by the mailbox limit. Stream deliveries are bounded by what Flow has
        pulled, outside the mailbox cap (@plan.Operations.O4); a released session's undelivered
        items are discarded with evidence, and a timer of an ended activation is discarded
        (@plan.Machines.O5).

        A refused delivery is kept, never lost. An ending whose turn is refused (InvalidTurn), or
        whose outcome mapper throws at admission, is delivered once more after the mail already
        queued; the second refusal is final and its issue names the cause.

        What was refused twice stays with what owes it:

        - a sent write's ending is parked on its lane and kept, blocking its key, until heard,
          cancelled, released or a local life ends;
        - a read's answer or a stream's failure is stranded, named, until that life asks again, ends
          or a boot;
        - a decline is kept on the lane of the key it asked for and blocks nothing, a newer decline
          replacing it.

        A kept ending gets one try per boot or adoption; the machine stays in the state that was
        waiting for it, with no fallback alternative and no API for it. A boot forgets a stranded
        read: a machine with a recurring schedule asks again, and one without stays where it was.
        Stated gap: a kept ending's one try and a stranded read let go leave no evidence today.

        React's pre-attach handles and their send buffer are @plan.React.O2 and @plan.React.O3.

        An action send(ref, event) and a handle's send(event) deliver a public event as a later turn
        of the target, after the sender's turn commits. A send never creates an actor or adopts a
        lazy entry, and may restore one from its record.

        By target:

        - live (used, kept by work in flight, or in the grace) is delivered, and a send is not a
          use;
        - ending and persisted is queued for the next incarnation;
        - ending and not persisted is dropped as SendTargetAbsent { reason: "NoRecord" };
        - ended, persisted, with a record that decodes is restored and delivered last, ending after
          the grace unless the event starts work in flight;
        - a record that does not decode is dropped with RecordUndecodable;
        - no record (not persisted, never created, disposed, forgotten) is dropped with NoRecord;
        - a lazy entry not adopted is dropped with NotAdopted;
        - a persisted actor while the Runtime boots is queued (@O2);
        - an actor being disposed by `disposeActor` is dropped with NoRecord.

        A full target is AdmissionOverloaded. Reasons are reported on the sender and never roll back
        its turn. Several sends keep their order.

        Every publication is one complete cut: a reader never sees an actor's new snapshot with old
        store data or the reverse. A new snapshots subscriber gets current truth first; slow
        subscribers may skip intermediate snapshots. Snapshots expose no revision numbers or raw
        Cause; each belongs to one store revision, which passive reads of it use.

        A snapshots subscription completes when its subscriber stops, or after the terminal snapshot
        of `disposeActor`, forget or Runtime disposal; on an identity with no actor it emits that
        identity's current snapshot and waits as a use. Reference:
        references/dispatch-desk/features/Tickets/editor/editor.machine.ts.
    O5  Context propagation
        A consumer always reads one complete, consistent snapshot of the context it depends on, and
        a bad context graph is refused before any work runs.

        Provider.select(selector) reads one complete provider snapshot. A context slot binds to one
        provider for the consumer's lifetime. Context reads form an acyclic upward graph; a cycle
        (ContextCycle), a missing provider (MissingContextProvider) or an incompatible one rejects
        at admission.

        Baselines are silent: `onContext` fires for none established at admission, restore, lazy
        adoption or hydration; an actor returning after an end gets one wave if a provider's
        selection differs from the one it ended with in this Runtime.

        A context change enqueues the consumer's declared event; it never runs a transition, memory
        patch or action directly, and never resets memory. Memory that belongs to one user,
        workspace or account is keyed into the actor's identity (its input fields), or lives in a
        Runtime per scope (@O7). A bound context consumer is a use of its provider.

        When a provider changes, each affected consumer receives at most one context update per
        wave, computed from the latest complete provider snapshots, and never a mix of new and stale
        values from the same cause (diamonds included).

        Slots compare with Equal.equals; an equal value causes no update, and consumers waiting on
        that branch continue. A context update keeps its place in the FIFO queue; later events never
        overtake it.

        A consumer selector that throws keeps that consumer's last valid context, publishes one
        issue, and retries only on a newer provider snapshot; the provider and other consumers are
        unaffected. Persistence capture never observes a half-propagated wave; it waits for the
        wave to settle (@plan.Persistence.O3).
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/features/Tickets/queue/queue.machine.ts.
    O6  Lifecycle and disposal
        Disposing an actor or a Runtime cleans up truthfully: unfinished writes are kept and
        reported, never silently dropped.

        Public lifecycle is active, disposing or disposed. runtime.disposeActor(actor) disposes the
        actor for every user: it removes the actor's record, keeps its non-Idle lanes as unbound
        lane records, and flushes the removal at once; an unbound lane record it leaves ends at an
        adopting ensure (or with its owner's cancel), listed as unbound under the identity and
        reported at each boot until then. `disposeActor` and forget stop a send's restore only once
        their removal is acknowledged; after a crash before that, the identity behaves as persisted:
        a plain send restores it, and with no use it ends after the grace.

        Disposing a provider while consumers are bound rejects with BlockedByDependents naming them.
        Actor and Runtime disposal are idempotent: repeated calls share one result.

        Once Runtime disposal starts, ready, `ensureActor`, `createActor` and `runPromise` reject
        with RuntimeDisposed. Runtime disposal closes admission first, then disposes consumers
        before providers; once a provider starts closing, no consumer can bind to it.

        forget removes the identity's record (retired, old-version or undecodable), so the next
        ensure starts fresh from its input; its parked and boot-queued endings become evidence, and
        the record's other boot mail goes silently, as after `disposeActor`. forget drops no hold: a
        Held lane stays an unbound lane, listed and reported at each boot, and a later ending stays
        on it, until its owner's cancel or an adopting ensure; a hold whose operation no longer
        exists is dropped, with evidence HoldForgotten (proposed name, the runner's ruling). forget
        of a live actor disposes it first.

        A lane is unbound while its identity has no decoded actor record and no incarnation.

        On disposal, queued events that have not started are dropped without running. Schedules
        cancel, placements stop, and stream sessions release within the bound (5 s, ReleaseOverdue);
        a new session waits for its releasing predecessor.

        Disposal invokes no new commit and drops no lane: the Runtime keeps the lanes, applies their
        endings and invokes what the lane table promotes (@plan.Operations.O3);
        runtime.unsettledWrites() lists non-Idle lanes by identity, for a host's beforeunload.

        Runtime disposal is bounded:

        - from its start nothing is invoked;
        - a queued commit that was never sent ends NotSent { Disposed }, delivered after reload for
          a persisted identity;
        - a call that settles within 5 seconds is parked;
        - past the bound an Attempt still on the wire ends unknown { Disposed } and its late result
          is ignored.

        Wherever endings of one lane are delivered or parked together, an ending that leaves a hold
        comes last: each queued NotSent { Disposed } before the head's unknown. An unknown keeps its
        head Held on disk and a definite ending drops it; a kill before the final capture restores
        unknown { Restored }, after it unknown { Disposed } is parked; the store hears the parked
        reason at the restore.

        No module starts work once disposal starts. A cleanup failure rejects dispose with
        CleanupFailed, quarantines the ref (later ensures report the same failure) and keeps the
        full Cause; it is never reported as success.

        Runtime disposal waits for the final persistence write within the same 5-second budget; a
        write still pending then reports StorageFailure (@plan.Persistence.O3). The terminal
        snapshot keeps the last committed state and memory; disposal runs no machine event.

        runtime.dispose({ forget: true }) also deletes the scope's stored records
        (@plan.Persistence.O5); plain dispose keeps them.
        Reference: references/dispatch-desk/app/host.ts.
    O7  Isolation
        Runtimes share no mutable state: actors, store, services, storage, clock or evidence.
        Handles from one Runtime are rejected by another; nested React providers use the nearest
        Runtime. A different user or account is a new Runtime with a different scope
        (@plan.React.O1).

        Every server request and Story run constructs its own Runtime; a module-level server Runtime
        is never used. Story storage is always fresh memory storage (@plan.Testing.O3). Reference:
        references/dispatch-desk/next/providers.tsx.
decisions
    D1  The app is a closed list of machines
        Lazy entries name machines the app already declares; nothing registers at runtime, so the
        structural representation stays complete for tools.
    D2  Wiring lives on refs and app defaults, never at call sites
        One place declares where an actor's context comes from, so tools can draw the graph.
constraints
    C1  One execution path
        Hosts, requests, React, Stories and the CLI use the same Runtime; fixtures replace
        services, never the kernel.
    C2  Identities are never reused
        Actor incarnations, request generations and Attempt ids never wrap or repeat within a
        Runtime or across restore; exhausting a counter refuses new work with IdentityExhausted
        before any change.
verification
    V1  Construction: closed-list errors, an unambiguous identity encoding (fields in name order,
        strings), inert constructors, Compiled refusals.
    V2  Services: a missing adapter service is a type error and a boot refusal by name; each
        service is acquired once.
    V3  Readiness: storage before validation, shared failure, interrupted waiter, second-tab
        refusal, no partial handles after failure, the boot send queue.
    V4  Identity and lifetime: a value-named ref given input is a type error and a mismatched ref
        - and input cannot be written;
        - conflicting refs reject;
        - a new incarnation adopts its identity's write lanes and receives no other late fact of the
          old one;
        - the end after the grace on the TestClock;
        - Strict Mode inside it;
        - a chain of two or more writes and reads in one incarnation;
        - schedules waiting without a use.
    V5  Mailboxes: the 1,024 and 64 bounds, invalid events, FIFO and rotation, send results
        owned by the sender, refused deliveries kept.
    V6  Context: diamond and chain waves give one update from latest values; a throwing selector
        is isolated.
    V7  Disposal and isolation: provider-with-consumers rejection, idempotent dispose, the
        5-second bound with unknown { Disposed } and the parking order, forget keeping holds,
        quarantine on cleanup failure, no sharing between Runtimes.
questions
    Q1  Can one services Layer be acquired storage-first?
        Recommended: prove it against Effect 4.0.5 in S1; if not, acquire the whole Layer before
        restore validation.
    Q2  Deferred to build time
        Id byte encoding; counter headroom sizes; evidence ordering details; lifecycle evidence
        causes; the private shutdown budget split.
